<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>3.11. Fourier Transforms</title>

    <alert>
        MTH 401
    </alert>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            You may recall the <term>Fourier transform</term> of a function
            <m>f(x)</m> had the form
            <md>
                {\mathcal{F}[f] = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)e^{i\omega x}~dx}.
            </md>
            It is thus useful to be able to solve that integral.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-fourier-complex">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>f(x)</m> is a rational function
                <m>{f(x) = \frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}}</m> with
                <m>{\text{deg}(Q) \ge \text{deg}(P)+1}</m>, and
                <m>{\omega \gt 0}</m> then
                <md>
                    \int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}e^{i\omega x}~dx = 2\pi i \sum\limits_{z_j \text{ sings in upper half plane}} \text{Res}_{z_j} \frac{P(z)}{Q(z)}e^{i\omega z}
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <remark>
        <p>
            That is, as long as the degree of the denominator is sufficiently
            larger than that of the numerator, instead of solving the integral
            restricted to the real plane, we can turn to complex land, find the
            singularities of <m>\ds \frac{P(z)}{Q(z)}</m> in the upper half
            plane, find the residues of those singularities for
            <m>\ds {\frac{P(z)}{Q(z)}e^{i\omega z}}</m>, then plug in and sum!
        </p>
    </remark>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A specific fourier transform example for <m>\omega = 2</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the integral
                    <md>
                        \int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac {e^{i2x}}{1+x^2}~dx,
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What about the more general Fourier transform
                    <md>
                        \int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac {e^{i\omega x}}{1+x^2}~dx,
                    </md>
                    where <m>\omega &gt; 0</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate the integral
                <md>
                    \int_{-\infty}^\infty \frac{x\sin x}{4+x^2}~dx =
  \text{Imag}\left\{  \int_{-\infty}^\infty \frac{xe^{ix}}{4+x^2}~dx\right\}.
                </md>
                That is, solve the Fourier transform first, then take the
                imaginary part of your answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>